::: {.cell}

Benign neoplasms are characterized by well-differentiated cells resembling their tissue of origin, uniform nuclear features, normal nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, rare mitoses, preserved architecture, and well-defined/encapsulated margins. Types include adenoma (glandular), papilloma (epithelial with fibrovascular cores), polyps (macroscopic term), cystadenoma (cystic glandular), hibernoma (brown fat), teratoma (germ cell-derived), hamartoma (disorganized normal tissue), choristoma (ectopic normal tissue), and mixed tumors (e.g., pleomorphic adenoma).
